// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// womb/kernel/arch/wasm32/sched_hal.vuma
// VWK Wasm32 Cooperative Scheduling HAL (PMT-pure).
// Wave R4d.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// Wasm32 uses cooperative scheduling (no preemption). The host event loop
// calls wasm_sched_loop periodically. For asyncify-based preemption, see
// K13+.
//
// ── Why cooperative? ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Wasm32 (the WebAssembly backend) is a stack-based VM that runs inside
// a host (browser, JS runtime, or standalone wasm runtime like Wasmtime
// / Wasmer). The wasm spec has no concept of:
//   - hardware interrupts (no IDT, no GIC, no PLIC)
//   - timer-driven preemption (no per-CPU tick the wasm guest can hook)
//   - privileged modes (no user/kernel split; all wasm code is "user")
// So the canonical R4a+b scheduler surface — designed around a timer IRQ
// that calls sched_tick + schedule() — does NOT map directly onto wasm.
// The closest wasm-native analogue is cooperative scheduling: each task
// runs until it voluntarily yields (via the host_yield extern below),
// at which point the host event loop picks the next task.
//
// ── The host_yield contract ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `host_yield()` is a wasm import (declared as `extern "C" fn host_yield()`
// below — VUMA's `extern "C"` block is the canonical way to declare a
// foreign import; the wasm32 backend lowers this to a wasm `import`
// declaration in the import section of the wasm module). The host
// provides the implementation:
//   - Browser: a `yield()` JS function that returns control to the
//     browser event loop (typically `await new Promise(r =>
//     setTimeout(r, 0))`), then resumes the wasm module.
//   - Wasmtime / Wasmer: a host function that returns immediately
//     (cooperative stub — the host has no event loop to yield to in
//     this scenario; the wasm guest just calls yield at safe points
//     to give the host a chance to inject other work).
//   - Hosted-mode x86_64 (this file's verification environment): the
//     x86_64 backend's __ffi_fallback_stub returns 0 — host_yield is
//     a no-op. The wasm_sched_loop self-test (main) just returns 0;
//     the loop body is exercised only when the file is compiled +
//     IVE-verified, not when the binary is executed.
//
// ── wasm_sched_loop algorithm ───────────────────────────────────────────────
// The loop is the cooperative analogue of the bare-metal timer-IRQ
// trampoline (R4a+b's scheduler caller pattern):
//   1. next = schedule(rq, tbl)         — pick min-vruntime task
//   2. if next == 256 (empty): return   — no runnable tasks, idle
//   3. sched_restore_context(tbl, next, tf)  — load next's saved PC/SP/status
//                                              into the shared TrapFrame
//   4. host_yield()                     — yield back to the host; the host
//                                              resumes us when ready
//   5. sched_save_context(tbl, next, tf)    — on resume, save the (still-
//                                              live) context back to the
//                                              task slot
//   6. sched_enqueue(rq, tbl, next)     — re-enqueue the yielded task
//   7. goto 1
//
// The "still-live context" assumption in step 5 is the cooperative-
// scheduling invariant: because the wasm VM never preempted us, the
// TrapFrame's pc/sp/status fields still describe the SAME task that was
// restored in step 3 (host_yield returned control to us at the wasm
// instruction right after the call, not into a different task). On a
// preemptive system this would be wrong (the OS could have switched
// tasks behind our back during the yield), but on wasm32 it's correct.
//
// ── Why does the loop run at most once in hosted mode? ──────────────────────
// The contract specifies: "In hosted mode: just loop once and return —
// host_yield is a stub." This is because:
//   - The x86_64 backend's __ffi_fallback_stub makes host_yield a no-op
//     (xor eax, eax; ret → returns 0 immediately). If wasm_sched_loop
//     called host_yield in a tight loop with no break, it would spin
//     forever (the no-op never gives control back to a scheduler that
//     could change the runqueue).
//   - The hosted-mode wasm_sched_loop is exercised by the file's self-
//     test (main returns 0 without calling wasm_sched_loop) — the loop
//     body itself is verified by the IVE Pass + compile step, not by
//     execution.
//   - K13+ (asyncify-based preemption) will replace the single-iteration
//     stub with a real wasm-side loop that uses Binaryen's asyncify pass
//     to suspend/resume the wasm stack. Until then, the hosted-mode loop
//     is one iteration.
//
// ── Layouts re-declared (no import in VUMA) ─────────────────────────────────
// VUMA has no `import` (Open Work §7), so this file re-declares:
//   - TrapFrame byte-identically to R2a's womb/kernel/trap/trap_frame.vuma
//   - Runqueue + ProcessTable byte-identically to R4a+b's
//     womb/kernel/proc/scheduler.vuma
//   - pt_get_state / pt_set_state / pt_get_vruntime / pt_set_vruntime /
//     pt_get_next / pt_set_next byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma
//     (the minimal subset the cooperative loop needs)
//   - schedule / sched_enqueue / sched_restore_context / sched_save_context
//     byte-identically to R4a+b's scheduler.vuma (re-declared locally so
//     wasm_sched_loop can call them without depending on a sibling file
//     at compile time).
//
// The byte-identical-redeclaration invariant (K2c "extern signatures
// MUST stay byte-identical" — extended to layouts in K3d) extends to
// layouts: this file's `wasm_sched_loop(rq, tbl, tf)` MUST see the same
// field offsets, field names, and field types as R2a's trap_frame.vuma
// and R4a+b's scheduler.vuma.
//
// ── PMT discipline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NO `*ptr`, `&x`, `allocate`, or `free` anywhere in this file. Both
// TrapFrame, Runqueue, and ProcessTable are ___pmt_buffer-backed
// State<T>'s allocated by the CALLER via `state_new(...)`. The State<T>
// params are regular VUMA-fn borrows (NOT extern #[borrow] — VUMA fn
// calls don't need the attribute, only extern "C" calls do). The only
// extern in this file is host_yield (which takes no State<T> params),
// so the codegen's conservative State-invalidation-on-extern-call
// doesn't fire on the loop's State<T> references — host_yield is
// declared before wasm_sched_loop, and the conservative invalidation
// only invalidates State<T>'s that were live ACROSS an extern call
// (here, the post-host_yield sched_save_context reads the same tbl
// and tf that were live before the yield, so the invalidation
// would force a reload — which is exactly what we want, since the
// host might have mutated them).
//
// ── Decimal-constants discipline ────────────────────────────────────────────
// All non-zero constants in this file use DECIMAL per the project-wide
// convention (see kernel-developer-guide.md §9):
//     512  = max syscalls (not used here, but listed for consistency)
//     256  = runqueue "empty" sentinel + gpr/fpu array size in TrapFrame
//     8    = bytes per u64 (loop bound for pack/unpack)
//     255  = 0xFF (byte mask for the unpack-shift-and-truncate path)
//     24   = _pad array size in TrapFrame (byte-loop bound)
//     2    = READY state value (set by sched_enqueue on the new task)
//     1    = RUNNING state value (set by sched_dequeue on the picked task)
//     0    = FREE state value (set by tf_init and state_new's zero-fill)
// Decimal avoids the parser's hex-literal path (parse_int_radix width-
// extension subtleties — see womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for
// the same rationale).
//
// ── Build & verify (hosted x86_64) ──────────────────────────────────────────
//     . "$HOME/.cargo/env" && CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 \
//         cargo build --profile release-fast --bin compile_dump
//     ./target/release-fast/compile_dump \
//         womb/kernel/arch/wasm32/sched_hal.vuma \
//         /tmp/wasm_sched_hal.bin x86_64 --verify
//     /tmp/wasm_sched_hal.bin ; echo "exit=$?"
// Expected: IVE Pass, exit=0 (the main() stub returns 0; the loop body
// is verified by IVE + compile, not by execution — see "Why does the
// loop run at most once in hosted mode?").

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Layouts (re-declared — no import in VUMA).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Canonical superset TrapFrame (576 bytes total) — re-declared byte-
// identically to R2a's womb/kernel/trap/trap_frame.vuma. See R2a's header
// for the field semantics (gpr 32×u64 LE-packed in 256 bytes; pc/status/
// vector/error_code/sp are 5 scalar u64 fields; fpu is 32×u64 LE-packed
// in 256 bytes; _pad is 24 alignment bytes).
layout TrapFrame = {
    gpr: [u8; 256],
    pc: u64,
    status: u64,
    vector: u64,
    error_code: u64,
    sp: u64,
    fpu: [u8; 256],
    _pad: [u8; 24],
}

// Runqueue: head/tail are Task indices (0..255) or 256 (empty). count is
// the number of tasks currently in the runqueue (0..256). The next-link
// field lives in ProcessTable.nexts (not here). need_resched is the
// W11S2 preemption flag (set by sched_tick when the current task's
// vruntime drifts > PREEMPT_THRESHOLD above the runqueue min — unused
// on wasm32 since wasm is cooperative-only, but the field is present
// to keep this layout byte-identical to R4a+b's scheduler.vuma).
// Re-declared byte-identically to R4a+b's scheduler.vuma.
layout Runqueue = {
    head: u32,
    tail: u32,
    count: u32,
    need_resched: u32,
}

// ProcessTable: re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma and
// R4a+b's scheduler.vuma. 10 fields (9 parallel flat [u8; N] arrays +
// count u32) totaling 14340 bytes. See K4a's task.vuma header "Why
// parallel flat byte arrays?" for the rationale (VUMA has no struct-
// array support). The wasm32 HAL only needs the states / vruntimes /
// nexts sub-arrays (the minimal subset for the cooperative loop), but
// the full layout is re-declared byte-identically so the field offsets
// match what the R4a+b scheduler sees.
layout ProcessTable = {
    pids: [u8; 1024],
    ppids: [u8; 1024],
    states: [u8; 256],
    prios: [u8; 256],
    vruntimes: [u8; 2048],
    mm_roots: [u8; 2048],
    fs_roots: [u8; 2048],
    fds: [u8; 2048],
    nexts: [u8; 2048],
    count: u32,
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// TrapFrame helpers (re-declared from R2a, byte-identical).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// tf_init + tf_get_gpr + tf_set_gpr, byte-identical to R2a. The wasm32
// HAL uses tf_init to zero the shared TrapFrame before the first
// wasm_sched_loop call; tf_get_gpr / tf_set_gpr are re-declared for
// completeness (the cooperative loop only reads/writes the scalar pc /
// sp / status fields via sched_save_context / sched_restore_context,
// but the helpers are part of the canonical R2a surface and re-declared
// here so any downstream wasm32 code that wants them has them in scope).

// Read the u64 GPR at canonical slot `idx` from tf.gpr. 8-byte LE unpack
// at byte offset `idx * 8`. Byte-identical to R2a's tf_get_gpr.
fn tf_get_gpr(tf: State<TrapFrame>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tf.gpr[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `val` to canonical slot `idx` in tf.gpr. 8-byte LE pack
// at byte offset `idx * 8`. Byte-identical to R2a's tf_set_gpr.
fn tf_set_gpr(tf: State<TrapFrame>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tf.gpr[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Zero every field of a caller-allocated TrapFrame. Byte-identical to
// R2a's tf_init.
fn tf_init(tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    let i = 0;
    while i < 256 { tf.gpr[i] = 0; i = i + 1; }
    tf.pc = 0;
    tf.status = 0;
    tf.vector = 0;
    tf.error_code = 0;
    tf.sp = 0;
    let j = 0;
    while j < 256 { tf.fpu[j] = 0; j = j + 1; }
    let k = 0;
    while k < 24 { tf._pad[k] = 0; k = k + 1; }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ProcessTable helpers (re-declared from K4a's task.vuma, byte-identical).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Minimal subset: state, vruntime, next. The cooperative loop uses:
//   pt_get_state / pt_set_state   (called by sched_enqueue / sched_dequeue)
//   pt_get_vruntime / pt_set_vruntime (called by sched_dequeue to pick min)
//   pt_get_next / pt_set_next     (called by sched_enqueue / sched_dequeue
//                                  to thread the runqueue linked list)
// The other 6 helpers (pid/ppid get/set, prio get/set, mm_root/fs_root/
// fds get/set) are NOT re-declared here — the wasm32 HAL doesn't need
// them. R4a+b's scheduler.vuma re-declares the full 12-helper set; K4d's
// fork/exec will re-declare the rest.

// Read the u8 `state` field of Task slot `idx` from ProcessTable.states.
fn pt_get_state(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u8 {
    return tbl.states[idx];
}

// Write the u8 `state` field of Task slot `idx` into ProcessTable.states.
fn pt_set_state(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u8) {
    tbl.states[idx] = val;
}

// Read the u64 `vruntime` field of Task slot `idx` from
// ProcessTable.vruntimes. 8-byte LE unpack loop.
fn pt_get_vruntime(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.vruntimes[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `vruntime` field of Task slot `idx` into
// ProcessTable.vruntimes. 8-byte LE pack loop.
fn pt_set_vruntime(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.vruntimes[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Read the u64 `next` field of Task slot `idx` from ProcessTable.nexts.
fn pt_get_next(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.nexts[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `next` field of Task slot `idx` into ProcessTable.nexts.
fn pt_set_next(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.nexts[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Scheduler helpers (re-declared from R4a+b's scheduler.vuma, byte-identical).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The cooperative loop calls: sched_init, sched_enqueue, sched_dequeue,
// schedule, sched_save_context, sched_restore_context. All 6 are re-
// declared byte-identically to R4a+b so wasm_sched_loop can call them
// without a sibling file at compile time. (K11 may consolidate these
// once VUMA gains an import mechanism.)

// sched_init: initialize an empty runqueue. Idempotent.
fn sched_init(rq: State<Runqueue>) {
    rq.head = 256;
    rq.tail = 256;
    rq.count = 0;
}

// sched_enqueue: add task `task_idx` to the tail of the runqueue. Sets
// the task's state to READY (2). Bumps rq.count.
fn sched_enqueue(rq: State<Runqueue>, tbl: State<ProcessTable>, task_idx: u32) {
    if rq.head == 256 {
        rq.head = task_idx;
        rq.tail = task_idx;
        pt_set_next(tbl, task_idx, 256);
    } else {
        pt_set_next(tbl, rq.tail, task_idx);
        rq.tail = task_idx;
        pt_set_next(tbl, task_idx, 256);
    }
    rq.count = rq.count + 1;
    pt_set_state(tbl, task_idx, 2);
}

// sched_dequeue: remove and return the task with the LOWEST vruntime
// (CFS "pick next" step). Returns 256 if the runqueue is empty.
fn sched_dequeue(rq: State<Runqueue>, tbl: State<ProcessTable>) -> u32 {
    if rq.head == 256 {
        return 256;
    }
    let min_idx = rq.head;
    let min_vruntime = pt_get_vruntime(tbl, min_idx);
    let cur = pt_get_next(tbl, min_idx);
    let prev_of_min = 256;
    let prev = min_idx;
    while cur != 256 {
        let v = pt_get_vruntime(tbl, cur as u32);
        if v < min_vruntime {
            min_idx = cur as u32;
            prev_of_min = prev;
        }
        prev = cur as u32;
        cur = pt_get_next(tbl, cur as u32);
    }
    if min_idx == rq.head {
        rq.head = pt_get_next(tbl, min_idx) as u32;
        if rq.head == 256 {
            rq.tail = 256;
        }
    } else {
        let min_next = pt_get_next(tbl, min_idx);
        pt_set_next(tbl, prev_of_min, min_next);
        if min_idx == rq.tail {
            rq.tail = prev_of_min;
        }
    }
    rq.count = rq.count - 1;
    pt_set_state(tbl, min_idx, 1);
    return min_idx;
}

// schedule: pick the next task to run. CFS "pick min vruntime". Returns
// the task index, or 256 if no tasks are runnable.
fn schedule(rq: State<Runqueue>, tbl: State<ProcessTable>) -> u32 {
    return sched_dequeue(rq, tbl);
}

// sched_save_context: copy tf.{pc, sp, status} into the Task slot's
// saved-context overload fields (mm_root, fs_root, fds). See R4a+b's
// scheduler.vuma header "Saved-context overload" section for the field
// mapping and the safety rationale.
fn sched_save_context(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, task_idx: u32, tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    pt_set_mm_root(tbl, task_idx, tf.pc);      // saved PC → mm_root
    pt_set_fs_root(tbl, task_idx, tf.sp);      // saved SP → fs_root
    pt_set_fds(tbl, task_idx, tf.status);      // saved status → fds
}

// sched_restore_context: copy the Task slot's saved-context overload
// fields (mm_root, fs_root, fds) back into tf.{pc, sp, status}.
fn sched_restore_context(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, task_idx: u32, tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    tf.pc = pt_get_mm_root(tbl, task_idx);     // mm_root → saved PC
    tf.sp = pt_get_fs_root(tbl, task_idx);     // fs_root → saved SP
    tf.status = pt_get_fds(tbl, task_idx);     // fds → saved status
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The 3 saved-context overload helpers (mm_root / fs_root / fds) — re-
// declared byte-identically to R4a+b's scheduler.vuma. (K4a's task.vuma
// declares them too; we need them locally because sched_save_context /
// sched_restore_context call them and VUMA has no import.)
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn pt_get_mm_root(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.mm_roots[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

fn pt_set_mm_root(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.mm_roots[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

fn pt_get_fs_root(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.fs_roots[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

fn pt_set_fs_root(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.fs_roots[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

fn pt_get_fds(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.fds[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

fn pt_set_fds(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.fds[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// host_yield extern (the wasm import).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// host_yield() is the cooperative-scheduling yield primitive. On wasm32
// it lowers to a wasm import; on hosted x86_64 it resolves to the
// __ffi_fallback_stub (no-op, returns 0). The wasm_sched_loop body
// calls host_yield between sched_restore_context and sched_save_context
// to give the host a chance to inject other work (browser event loop,
// other wasm instances, etc.).
//
// No #[borrow] attribute needed — host_yield takes no State<T> params
// and returns nothing. The codegen's conservative State-invalidation-
// on-extern-call will invalidate any State<T> live across the call,
// which is exactly what we want (the post-yield sched_save_context
// reads tbl and tf as if they might have been mutated by the host).
extern "C" {
    // host_yield() — yield back to the host event loop. The host resumes
    // the wasm module at the instruction after the call when ready.
    // Browser: setTimeout-based yield. Wasmtime: immediate return.
    // Hosted x86_64: __ffi_fallback_stub (no-op).
    fn host_yield();
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// wasm_sched_loop: the cooperative scheduling loop.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// See the file header "wasm_sched_loop algorithm" for the 7-step loop.
// In hosted mode, the loop runs AT MOST ONCE then returns — host_yield
// is a no-op stub, so an unbounded loop would spin forever. K13+ will
// replace this with a real asyncify-based preemptive loop.
//
// State<T> params: 3 (rq + tbl + tf). All three are read AND written
// across the loop body (rq via sched_enqueue, tbl via sched_save_context
// + sched_restore_context, tf via sched_restore_context + sched_save_
// context). The `current` local holds the task index that was just
// restored (so we know which task to save back into on resume).
fn wasm_sched_loop(rq: State<Runqueue>, tbl: State<ProcessTable>, tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    // Single-iteration stub for hosted mode — see file header "Why does
    // the loop run at most once in hosted mode?". K13+ will replace
    // this `let iter = 0; while iter < 1 { ... iter = iter + 1; }`
    // wrapper with the real cooperative loop (the inner loop body
    // below is the algorithm the contract specifies).
    let iter = 0;
    while iter < 1 {
        // Step 1: pick the next task.
        let next = schedule(rq, tbl);
        // Step 2: if the runqueue is empty, return.
        if next == 256 {
            return;
        }
        // Step 3: restore the next task's saved context into the shared
        // TrapFrame (tf.pc / tf.sp / tf.status ← tbl's saved-context
        // overload fields).
        sched_restore_context(tbl, next, tf);
        // Step 4: yield back to the host. On wasm32 this is the
        // cooperative yield; on hosted x86_64 this is a no-op stub.
        host_yield();
        // Step 5: on resume, save the (still-live) context back to the
        // task slot. This is the cooperative-scheduling invariant —
        // see file header "wasm_sched_loop algorithm".
        sched_save_context(tbl, next, tf);
        // Step 6: re-enqueue the yielded task at the tail of the
        // runqueue (gives other tasks a chance to run on the next
        // iteration).
        sched_enqueue(rq, tbl, next);
        // Step 7: goto 1 (the while loop iterates — but in hosted mode
        // we break after 1 iteration).
        iter = iter + 1;
    }
    return;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Self-test (the R4d DoD).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The contract specifies `fn main() -> i32 { return 0; }` stub — the
// wasm_sched_loop body is verified by IVE Pass + compile, not by
// execution (see file header "Why does the loop run at most once in
// hosted mode?"). The stub returns 0 unconditionally.
fn main() -> i32 {
    return 0;
}
